      "math_academy_id": "q-213112",
      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1\\}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?\n\nThe domain is {{field-1}}\n\nThe range is \n$\\{y\\in $\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,:\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- The set of first components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the domain is $R.$\n\n- Notice that $0\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1\\le 2$ for all $x\\in R,$ and it is continuous on $R.$ So, the set of second components contains all real numbers greater than or equal to $0$ and less than or equal to $2$:\n\n$$\n\\{y\\in R\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nHence, the range is $\\{y\\in R\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
